> I've had a couple wizards get their scrolls torched by fire-trapped
> containers before they could get their luck up, so it seems like it
> might be worth some #untrapping then. 

The problem there is that wizards shouldn't be carrying their scrolls if
they're at all valuable.  There are a lot more things that can burn 
scrolls than just chest traps; magic traps come to mind, but there are
others.

In general, you want to drop scrolls somewhere 'safe' that's also
convenient for when you finally get a bag or other device.  This holds
true for potions as well, since they're affected by frost too.

> Also, according to wikihack the electric trap destroys all wands 
> and rings in your inventory, not just worn rings (I can't read source, 
> but it does mention destroying wands). 
> So it seems like #untrap might have some use for a zero-
> luck character.

"Greater than zero" use?  Yes.  But even the poor zero-luck character
has the odds so far in his favor that it's just not worth worrying
about.  Again, consider:

-- chest must be trapped (10%)
-- trap must not be dud (61%)
-- trap must be shock trap (a bit more than 6% because it appears on
	both the "good" and "bad" paths, call it 10% as likely to be
	close enough)

...And you're still only at .61%.  Couple this with some other factors;
ie. you should be maxing luck ASAP with the luckstone from Mines' End,
you should be grabbing the first bag you can, etc. etc. -- and you end
up with just no significant probability that you'll ever run into the
problem... so why bother?  (Also, you don't even have to max luck; you
just need to get luck of 7 or better, and the shock trap can't appear on
anything but the 'bad' path, which only shows up 1 in 20 times.)

Which sort of matches with my recollection, really.  I hadn't ever
thought about chest traps for a long, long time, and kind of wondered
why -- this would explain it.  I historically always maxed luck ASAP in
vanilla, because it provided so many other known benefits -- this just
happens to be yet another one of those.

